good to see you again facing an  increasingly tense standoff over ukraine  u.s president joe biden and russian  president vladimir putin spent two hours  engaged in intense talks he told  president putin directly that if russia  further invades ukraine the united  states and our european allies would  respond with strong economic measures  we would provide additional defensive  materiel to the ukrainians and we would  fortify our nato allies on the eastern  flank with additional capabilities in  recent weeks russia has amassed an  estimated 100 000 troops near the  ukraine border along with tanks and  artillery u.s intelligence believes  putin could be preparing to launch a  full-blown invasion as early as january  this build-up is concerning to us it is  still not  entirely clear what mr putin's  intentions are the issue is ukraine's  place in the world  russia wants assurances that the former  soviet state will not be allowed to join  nato or even cooperate with the western  alliance  the u.s has refused to accept such  demands biden says he fully supports  ukraine's sovereignty i'm not sure putin  really wants it part of russia so much  as he just doesn't want it part of the  west on top of promising additional  military aid the u.s has warned any  invasion would trigger severe economic  sanctions against russia moscow could be  cut off from the global financial system  but that has not deterred putin in the  past and some experts believe the  military buildup is simply a ploy  designed to make cooperation between  ukraine and the west seem too risky it  makes ukrainians perhaps want to join  nato more but i think it makes other  nato countries want to invite ukraine in  less  because we realize what that would mean  it would mean the possibility of war  against russia though neither leader  seemed to budge in their position the  white house did describe the  conversation as direct and  straightforward a sign perhaps that  diplomacy may be an achievable path to  